{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"lactose intolerance\" (+ develop develops developed onset) (+ adult adults adulthood later age aging))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#0_287163141","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q167","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#0_287163141\" , \"end_char\": 710.000000 , \"headings\": \"Mayo Clinic Q and A: <cover>Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age</cover>Mayo Clinic Q and A: Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age New SW Region VP for Mayo Clinic Health System In the Loop: Tips for Things Children Swallow \" , \"segment\": \"Mayo Clinic Q and A: Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age – Mayo Clinic News Network By Liza Torborg Mayo Clinic Q and A: Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age June 14, 2016 DEAR MAYO CLINIC: What would cause someone to become lactose-intolerant later in life? I’m in my 40s and have never had an issue with dairy, but, now, I can’t seem to have it without problems. Do I need to see a doctor to be tested for allergies, or should I just avoid dairy? ANSWER: Lactose intolerance isn’t a true allergy, and it can develop at any age. In some people, lactose intolerance may be triggered by another medical condition, such as Crohn’s "}}
