{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q167"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#1_287164453","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q167","rank":5,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#1_287164453\" , \"end_char\": 1264.000000 , \"headings\": \"Mayo Clinic Q and A: <cover>Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age</cover>Mayo Clinic Q and A: Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age New SW Region VP for Mayo Clinic Health System In the Loop: Tips for Things Children Swallow \" , \"segment\": \"Do I need to see a doctor to be tested for allergies, or should I just avoid dairy? ANSWER: Lactose intolerance isn’t a true allergy, and it can develop at any age. In some people, lactose intolerance may be triggered by another medical condition, such as Crohn’s disease. In others, it develops without a specific underlying cause. It would be a good idea to have your condition evaluated by your doctor to confirm that what you’re dealing with truly is lactose intolerance. Lactose intolerance results from a problem with the carbohydrate lactose, a type of sugar found in dairy products. When you eat or drink dairy products, enzymes in your small intestine digest lactose, so the body can "}}
