{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#3_287167645","relevance":3,"reason":"This Mayo Clinic segment is from a directly relevant article and explains that lactose intolerance can develop after illness, injury, or surgery affecting the small intestine, and also describes primary lactose intolerance developing as lactase production changes after childhood.","learned":"Lactose intolerance may develop over time as primary lactase production changes, or secondarily after small-intestine illness, injury, or surgery; some secondary cases may improve if the underlying disorder is treated."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q167","reason":"The query is still producing directly relevant medical evidence; continue to the next result to find more grade-3 documents, preferably independent sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q167"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#4_287169326","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q167","rank":9,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#4_287169326\" , \"end_char\": 2760.000000 , \"headings\": \"Mayo Clinic Q and A: <cover>Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age</cover>Mayo Clinic Q and A: Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age New SW Region VP for Mayo Clinic Health System In the Loop: Tips for Things Children Swallow \" , \"segment\": \"More common than secondary lactose intolerance is primary lactose intolerance. People who develop primary lactose intolerance start life making normal levels of lactase — a necessity for infants, who get all their nutrition from milk. As children replace milk with other foods, lactase production normally decreases. It stays high enough, though, to digest the amount of dairy in a typical adult diet. In primary lactose intolerance, lactase production declines below normal at some point for reasons that are unclear. The low amount of lactase then makes milk products difficult to digest and leads to lactose intolerance symptoms. Your doctor can confirm a diagnosis of lactose intolerance with a clinical test. One that’s often used is a lactose tolerance test. It assesses "}}
