{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_911964391#13_1975790367","relevance":2,"reason":"The MedicineNet article is broadly about lactose intolerance rather than specifically dedicated to whether it can develop over time, but the snippet provides direct supporting evidence that lactase can decrease after childhood into adulthood and that age of symptom appearance varies.","learned":"Adult-type hypolactasia is a genetically programmed decrease in lactase after childhood that persists into adulthood, and symptoms can appear at different ages."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q168","reason":"This clinical-synonym query may uncover more authoritative sources, and the first result contains useful adult-onset evidence even if not fully dedicated to the exact question."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q168"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_911964391#14_1975793656","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q168","rank":2,"snippet":"Diarrhea Digestive Disorders: Worst Foods for Digestion Indigestion (Dyspepsia, Upset Stomach Pain) Endoscopy (EGD) Procedure Hydrogen Breath Test Intestinal Gas (Belching, Bloating, Flatulence) Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) Lactose Tolerance Test Nausea and Vomiting Osteoporosis Slideshow Stomach Picture Stool Acidity Test Tummy Trouble Quiz \" , \"segment\": \"Genetically programmed causes of lactose intolerance The most common cause of lactase deficiency is a decrease in the amount of lactase that occurs after childhood and persists into adulthood, referred to as adult-type hypolactasia. This decrease in lactase is genetically programmed. <cover>Lactase deficiency (and lactose intolerance) is most common among Asians, affecting more than 90% of adults </cover>in some communities. People with ancestry from Northern Europe, on the other hand, have a 5% rate of lactase deficiency. In addition to variability in the prevalence of lactase deficiency among ethnic groups, there also is variability in the age at which symptoms of lactase deficiency (and lactose intolerance) appear. As people age they may develop lactose intolerance; however, the extent of the intolerance appears to be mild and not associated with clinical symptoms. Therefore, the development of lactose intolerance in the elderly should not be made lightly. Food Allergies vs. Food "}}
