{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_623993619#1_853701374","relevance":2,"reason":"The Wikipedia article is broadly about lactose intolerance rather than dedicated to whether it can develop over time, but it directly supports the answer by stating that primary lactose intolerance occurs as lactase declines with age and secondary lactose intolerance can follow small-intestine injury.","learned":"Primary lactose intolerance can occur as lactase declines with age; secondary lactose intolerance can result from small-intestine injury or disease."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"adult-onset lactose intolerance\" (+ develop developed \"later in life\" age adults))","reason":"Use the clinical phrase 'adult-onset lactose intolerance' to target dedicated medical pages or articles explaining whether lactose intolerance can appear later in life."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"adult-onset lactose intolerance\" (+ develop developed \"later in life\" age adults))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1464984130#9_2945917562","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q172","rank":1,"snippet":"and gas that result from consuming milk and other dairy products. 3 ﻿ There are several forms of lactose intolerance. Congenital Lactase Deficiency Congenital lactase deficiency (also called congenital alactasia) is a rare inherited form of lactose intolerance in which infants are unable to break down lactose in breast milk or formula and have severe diarrhea if they aren't given a lactose-free alternative. Congenital lactase deficiency is caused by mutations in the LCT gene that provides instructions for making the lactase enzyme. 5 ﻿ Lactase Non-persistence Lactase non-persistence is a common type of <cover>adult-onset lactose intolerance affecting around 65% of adults. </cover>It is caused by decreased expression (activity) of the LCT gene. Symptoms typically begin 30 minutes to two hours after ingesting dairy. 3 ﻿ Most people with lactase non-persistence retain some lactase activity and can continue to include some lactose in their diets, such as in the form of cheese or yogurt that tend to be tolerated better than fresh milk.\" , \"start_char\": 5278.000000 , \"title\": \"What are Digestive Enzymes? Types and Functions\" , \"url\": \"https://www.verywell.com/"}}
