{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_140887620#5_287170842","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the Mayo Clinic Q&A explicitly titled 'Lactose Intolerance Can Develop at Any Age.' Although the visible passage focuses on testing, the document is dedicated to the exact question and prior segments directly state the answer.","learned":"Duplicate/near-duplicate evidence: Mayo Clinic addresses lactose intolerance developing at any age and discusses diagnostic tests such as lactose tolerance and hydrogen breath tests."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"lactose intolerance\" nhs (+ develop developed \"later in life\" adult adults age))","reason":"Search specifically for NHS evidence about lactose intolerance developing later in life or in adults to add an independent authoritative source."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"lactose intolerance\" nhs (+ develop developed \"later in life\" adult adults age))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_656102323#12_1189448764","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q179","rank":1,"snippet":"surgery to your small intestine, or taking certain medication. Secondary lactase deficiency is the most common cause of lactose intolerance in the UK, particularly in babies and young children. Possible causes of secondary lactase deficiency include: gastroenteritis – an infection of the stomach and intestines coeliac disease – a bowel condition caused by an adverse reaction to a protein called gluten Crohn's disease – a long-term condition that causes inflammation of the lining of the digestive system ulcerative colitis – a long-term condition that affects the large intestine chemotherapy – a cancer treatment long courses of antibiotics The decrease in the production of lactase in secondary lactase deficiency is sometimes only temporary, but it may be permanent if it's caused by a long-term condition. It's also possible to develop secondary lactase deficiency <cover>later in life, even without another condition to trigger it. This is because your body's production of lactase naturally reduces as you get older.\" , \"start_char\": 6814.000000 , \"title\": \"Lactose intolerance - Illnesses & conditions | NHS </cover>inform\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/nutritional/lactose-intolerance\" }"}}
