{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_656102323#12_1189448764","relevance":2,"reason":"The NHS Inform page is a general lactose intolerance page rather than dedicated to the exact question, but the snippet directly supports the answer by stating that secondary lactase deficiency can develop later in life and that lactase production naturally reduces with age.","learned":"NHS Inform says secondary lactase deficiency can develop later in life, even without another trigger, because lactase production naturally reduces as people get older."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"can you become lactose intolerant\" (+ \"later in life\" adult adults age suddenly))","reason":"Use the exact consumer question phrasing to surface more dedicated articles that directly answer whether a person can become lactose intolerant over time or later in life."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"can you become lactose intolerant\" (+ \"later in life\" adult adults age suddenly))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1165021332#1_2357100937","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q180","rank":10,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1165021332#1_2357100937\" , \"end_char\": 2155.000000 , \"headings\": \"<cover>Can You Become Lactose Intolerant Later in Life?</cover>Can You Become Lactose Intolerant Later in Life? Digestive Problems Similar to Lactose Intolerance How to Determine if You're Lactose Intolerant How to Manage Different Degrees of Lactose Intolerance Editorial Sources and Fact-Checking NEWSLETTERS Sign up for our Digestive Health Newsletter! Enter your email \" , \"segment\": \"Thinkstock You might love milk, but as you age, sometimes milk stops loving you back. If you feel bloated and gassy or have cramps and nausea within a few hours of drinking milk or eating milk products, it could be lactose intolerance, says Ashkan Farhadi, MD, director of the Digestive Disease Center at MemorialCare Medical Group in Costa Mesa, California, and a gastroenterologist at Orange Coast Memorial Medical Center in Fountain Valley, California. Except for very rare instances, every newborn has the ability to make lactase, an enzyme that helps the small intestine digest lactose, the sugar found in milk, says Richard Grand, MD, professor of pediatrics at ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1165021332#0_2357098681","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_878424857#4_1315558013","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_878424857#0_1315551333","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_878424857#1_1315553156","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_471468274#0_816370558","rank":5,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_878424857#3_1315556443","rank":6,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_878424857#2_1315554861","rank":7,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_471468274#4_816376265","rank":8,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_471468274#3_816375003","rank":9,"relevance":3}]}}
